  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Square Roots and Perfect-Square Structure",
    "description": "Understand square roots, recognize perfect squares, and use number patterns to estimate and check roots.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach a square root as a number whose square gives the radicand, distinguishing the principal non-negative square root √n from the two solutions of x²=n when solving an equation. Define perfect squares and explain prime-factor pairs, unit-digit restrictions for integer squares, pairing digits into periods for the number of root digits, and bounding a root between consecutive integers. Use these patterns as checks and estimates, not as substitutes for exact calculation. Draw examples from the chapter such as √6084=78, √53824=232, and squares in a stated interval. Learners should recognize perfect-square structure, estimate a plausible root, and reject impossible unit-digit or digit-count choices without claiming every candidate is a square."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Square roots reverse squaring, but √n has a specific meaning: it is the principal, non-negative root. Recognizing square structure helps you estimate and verify a result.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If 12²=144, then √144=12. The radical symbol gives the non-negative root. The equation x²=144 has two solutions, 12 and −12, since both squares equal 144. Keep evaluation of a radical separate from solving a square equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
